# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[remove]] the hair from.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1808  |title=The Repertory of Patent Inventions|author=|page=90 |ISBN=|passage=Now know ye, that in compliance with the said proviso, we the said John Cant and John Millar do hereby declare that our said invention of a new method of tanning leather is described in the manner following : that our method of preparing hides and skins by liming, '''hairing''', fleshing, and baiting, is the same as that in use by the most experienced tanners; that is to say: All leather that is to be dressed or curried, we use the operation of what tanners call baiting, for this reason; that the leather when curried should carry a sufficient quantity of oil, and dry a good colour.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1825 |title=American Mechanics' Magazine - Volume 2 |author=|page=71 |ISBN=|passage=By his method raw hides, after '''hairing''' and baiting, are converted into leather in less than thirty hours.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1971 |title=The Reuben King Journal, 1800-1806 |author=Reuben King; Virginia S. Wood; Ralph V. Wood |page=49 |ISBN=|passage=I took 25 hides out of the Lime with Stephens help I '''haired''' them and fleshed them}}
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[grow]] hair (where there was a [[bald]] spot).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1863  |title=Yankee-notions - Volume 12 |author=|page= 312|ISBN=|passage=He has '''haired''' up and healed over.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1887 |title=National Stockman and Farmer - Volume 11 |author=|page=7 |ISBN=|passage=It has '''haired''' over nicely. There are no bad results from it in any way whatever that I can detect.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992  |title=Georgia Voices: Fiction |author=Hugh Ruppersburg |page=492 |ISBN=|passage=The bald patch on his hip was '''hairing''' over and he no longer limped. }}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To cause to have or [[bear]] hair; to provide with hair
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1937 |title=Dyestuffs - Volume 35, Issue 1 |author=|page=1 |ISBN=|passage=THE following classes of fiber are employed for '''hairing''' dolls : human hair, mohair, cross-bred wool, horsehair, hog-bristle, unspun cotton. Human hair is only used for '''hairing''' dolls of an extremely expensive class.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014 |title=The Making of George A. Romero's Day of the Dead|author=Lee Karr; Greg Nicotero |page= |ISBN=|passage=So they did three different sculptures and then ran the masks and painted them, '''haired''' them, and sent them out to us.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2017 |title=Wells Brothers: The Young Cattle Kings|author=Andy Adams |page= |ISBN=|passage=The winter had '''haired''' them like llamas, the sleet had worked no hardship, as a horse paws to the grass, and any concern for the outside saddle stock was needless.}}
# To string the [[bow]] for a [[violin]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1896  |title=The Bow, Its History, Manufacture & Use |author=Henry Saint-George |page=96 |ISBN=|passage=The bow is now '''haired''', and all that remains to make it ready for use is to rosin it.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1969  |title=Violin bow making |author=John Alfred Bolander |page=105 |ISBN=|passage=The tools used for '''hairing''' a bow by various reparimen can be unlimited in their selection. A bowmaker has a different attitude toward '''hairing''' than a repairman and this I believe reflects the type of the finished job that is done.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015  |title=The String Instrument Owner's Handbook |author=Michael J. Pagliaro |page= 108|ISBN=|passage=To '''hair''' a bow, a hank of horse hair (A) is selected and combed so that all hairs are parallel to each other. }}

